welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Sultan Sood Al Qasimi he is at Yale  as a visiting scholar in the Middle East  Studies Council and is teaching a course  on the politics of modern Middle Eastern  art Mr Al Qasimi is a columnist and  researcher on social political and  cultural affairs in the Arab Gulf states  he is also the founder of the barge eel  Art Foundation today we'll talk with Mr  Al Qasimi  about modern Middle Eastern art and  architecture welcome Sultan thank you  let's start with talking about the  course you're teaching here what are you  teaching the students well as you said  the New York production I'm teaching a  class on the politics of modern Middle  Eastern art we look at the 20th and the  21st century across the Middle East so  we looked at North Africa so we look at  the Arab world Egypt Syria Iraq all the  way to Morocco the Gulf states but we  also look at Israel Iran and Turkey and  we try and study the intersection of art  and politics how artists reacted to  major political events wars revolutions  but also to social movement moving women  emancipation movement labor movements  and whatnot and so we we try and study  this region through an artistic lens  where as it's been always studied  through a political lens okay and what  years are you looking at I know it's  modern but what does that mean in terms  of we really look at the early 20th  century there was a major event that  took place around 1906 where there was a  religious decree that said that art is  okay and art is allowed and it's no  longer forbidden and so you have the  advent of the first university that  teaches art in a systemic system in  Egypt in Cairo in early 20th century so  we almost take that as our starting  point and we go all the way to the Arab  Spring and well into the 21st century  okay so I would venture a guess that  most Westerners do  know much about modern Middle Eastern  art so what are some of the things that  you might point out as differences or  maybe even similarities that we wouldn't  be aware of I would say that most Middle  Easterners aren't aware of modern Middle  East or not it's such a a new  scholarship and the new you know the  space that we're still exploring it  people are surprised what I tell them  after 15 or 20 years of reading and  exploring Middle Eastern art that I  don't know about a certain artist  actually it makes me happy to discover  that there's so much that I do not know  and so I would say that a lot of people  aren't aware how active the women's  artists movement was throughout the  Middle East all the way back to the  early 20th century a lot of people  aren't aware of the ethnic minorities  religious minorities Jewish artists  Christian artists minorities ethnic  minorities how active they were and all  the cross-cultural exchange that the  region has had with South Asia India  with Africa sub-saharan Africa with the  Mediterranean Basin the Caucasus and so  it is such an eclectic part of the world  the people I think would really be you  know positively surprised to find out  how how varied it is and in terms of the  art itself is it paintings as well as  sculpture it runs the whole gamut yes oh  we do look at painting sculpture but  also film we look at photography poetry  there's so much to think of but of  course I have to I have to emphasize  that we're just touching upon these  subjects rather than really delving deep  into them okay and you recently created  an art foundation the bar geo Art  Foundation I'll talk a little bit about  that why you thought it was necessary to  establish it so I had been collecting  art since 2002 so it's a it's almost 17  years now and I always wanted to have a  home  and so I started collecting art and and  I lobbied my local government back home  in the emirate that I come from and they  had given me a space for a good eight  year period and now the works are on  long term display the Sharjah Art Museum  and Sharjah is the emirate in the UAE  where I come from and it's a beautiful  Museum dating back to the 1990s and they  have been very very generous with giving  me the space so that we can have a  long-term display and this is a  challenge Marilyn that we face in the  Arab world where middle-eastern art  isn't really on put on long-term display  we really suffer from this it should  called short-term exhibitions which is  wonderful but in reality you don't have  a chance to develop a long-term  relationship think of yourself when you  go when you went with maybe your parents  or your family to visit a museum  exhibition maybe at the MoMA and then  you go maybe you take your kids or you  take your nephews or your nieces and  then so it's a generational relationship  we don't have this in the Middle East  and so that's what I'm trying to do  right right you know in fact you  mentioned the MoMA I recently went there  with my daughter and I was very  surprised as part of one of their  exhibits they did have modern Middle  Eastern art exhibited there have you  seen that yes and they have a little  note about why it's so important to show  this art so I understand your point on  that precisely so how many how big how  many paintings are exhibited in the  museum oh right now the long-term  display at the Sharjah museum has about  a hundred and forty works although we  are now launching a few short-term  exhibitions coming here to the US and so  some of the works would be leaving the  museum and they will be exchanged over  the next year or so very good very good  and you mentioned your hometown and I  know you're working on a book project  about its architecture so let's talk up  a little bit about that and why it's so  important to you to to recognize this  architecture so I'm really interested in  modernism I'm interested in  documenting and archiving the near  history rather than the ancient history  1950s 60s 70s not only in art but also  in architecture and 